}
state.active = true;
...
const finishActivation = () => {
  if (checkCanFocusTrap) {
    updateTabbableNodes();
  }
  addListeners();
  if (onPostActivate) { onPostActivate(); }
};

Since they didn't pass checkCanFocusTrap option, checkCanFocusTrap is undefined, so false. Therefore !checkCanFocusTrap is true, so they call updateTabbableNodes() before setting state.active = true. Good. So tabbableGroups should be set correctly before activation.

Now after setting state.active = true, they define finishActivation and then if (checkCanFocusTrap) { ... } else { finishActivation(); }. Since checkCanFocusTrap is false, they go to else and call finishActivation() immediately. finishActivation calls addListeners() (which sets timeout) and runs onPostActivate (none). So after activate returns, focus is still on the button #open. Then after a timeout, tryFocus(getInitialFocusNode()) runs.

Now getInitialFocusNode at that time: doc.activeElement is still #open (since focus hasn't changed). containersContain(doc.activeElement) still false (button not inside dialog). So we go to else: firstTabbableGroup = state.tabbableGroups[0]; firstTabbableNode = firstTabbableGroup && firstTabbableGroup.firstTabbableNode; node = firstTabbableNode || getNodeForOption('fallbackFocus'); So we need to see what firstTabbableNode is.

The tabbableGroups are built from containers (only #address-dialog). Within that container, we call tabbable(container) which returns tabbable nodes. Let's compute: The container includes fieldset disabled (so its children are disabled). The fieldset disabled makes its contents not focusable. The tabbable function from upstream-dependency filters nodes using isNodeMatchingSelectorTabbable which checks disabled, hidden input, hidden, details-with-summary, and tabindex <0, and non-tabbable radio. Fieldset disabled will cause the elements inside to have disabled attribute? Actually fieldset disabled does not automatically disable its descendants; but the spec says that disabled on a fieldset disables all form controls within it, except those that are children of legend. However the implementation likely relies on the disabled property of the element itself. The fieldset element itself is disabled, but its children (label, select) are not disabled attributes. However many browsers treat fieldset disabled as disabling its descendants. Does the tabbable library check node.disabled? Yes, isNodeMatchingSelectorFocusable checks node.disabled. For the select #speed, its disabled property will be true because it's inside a disabled fieldset? In HTML, a fieldset with disabled attribute makes its form controls disabled, but the disabled property reflects that. So #speed.disabled should be true. Similarly the label is not a form control, but it's not focusable anyway. So #speed is disabled, thus not tabbable.

Thus the tabbable nodes inside container are: #street input (enabled), #save button, #cancel button. Also maybe the fieldset itself? fieldset is not focusable unless tabindex. So first tabbable node should be #street.

Thus getInitialFocusNode should return #street. Then tryFocus should focus it. Why didn't it? Possibly because the timeout didn't fire before the assertion ran? The test likely runs synchronously: after pressing Enter, they immediately check focus. The timeout is scheduled as macrotask, so focus change happens after the current task ends. The test's assertion runs immediately after the action, before the timeout fires, causing focus still on #open. That explains failure.

Thus fix: We need to ensure focus moves to #street synchronously when dialog opens. Options: set delayInitialFocus: false in the focus trap config, or call trap.activate({ delayInitialFocus: false })? Actually the focus-trap's activate method accepts activateOptions which can override config options like delayInitialFocus. In the click handler, we can pass { delayInitialFocus: false } to trap.activate. Or we can change the config when creating trap: set delayInitialFocus: false.

